A solid package of essays that would put you in the lower First Class territory (74). 
Your answer to Q1(a) was your weakest. Up to your discussion of Enoch, the essay is reasonable (though not impeccably clear). 
But the main claim about oppressed groups and democracy, however bold, is a bit outlandish and largely irrelevant in the context of a question on moral realism. 
Your answer to Q18 was your strongest. Excellent knowledge of the relevant literature, distinctions, and discussion. 
Your answer to Q12 was solid, though quite a few points were debatable. 
For instance, contrary to what you write, prioritarianism does not explain which and when inequality is bad; it just takes as given a welfare distribution, and advocates to weigh the worse off more. 
(Any other claim would be an add-on.) 
Also, you did not make clear why we should think it’s absurd to weigh the person at 1M more than the person at .999M; in particular, perhaps it’s odd to have a discontinuity between the two, but not all forms of prioritarianism have that (the difference could be very small, etc). 
The last section seems to suggest that Anderson is sympathetic to sufficientarianism, which is not right.
